CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to ensure the safety and order of underwater leisure activities, encourage underwater leisure activities, and promote the sound development of underwater leisure business.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  1. The term "underwater" means the region below the surface of seawater defined in subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Water-Related Leisure Activities Safety Act (hereinafter referred to as "surface of seawater") or the surface of inland waters defined in subparagraph 8 of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "surface of inland waters");
  2. The term "underwater leisure activities" means activities prescribed by Presidential Decree, including skin diving and scuba diving, as those performed for pastime, recreation, sports, education, etc. with craft or equipment for underwater leisure in the water;
  3. The term "person engaged in underwater leisure activities" means a person who is engaged in underwater leisure activities;
  4. The term "underwater leisure activity zone" means an area between the bounds prescribed by Presidential Decree for underwater leisure activities and the point where underwater leisure activities are conducted;
  5. The term "underwater leisure craft" means a ship under the Ship Act and a power-driven water leisure craft used for movement on the surface of seawater, on the surface of inland waters, or underwater for underwater leisure activities;
  6. The term "underwater leisure equipment" means devices or installations prescribed by Presidential Decree as necessary for underwater leisure activities, other than underwater leisure craft, such as diving masks, snorkels, air tanks, regulators, and buoyancy control devices;
  7. The term "underwater leisure facilities" means facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree, including propeller shields and dive ladders, as required for safety of underwater leisure craft;
  8. The term "underwater leisure business" means any of the following business related to underwater leisure activities:
  a. Business of renting underwater leisure craft or underwater leisure equipment to persons engaged in underwater leisure activities;
  b. Business of transporting persons engaged in underwater leisure activities by underwater leisure craft;
  c. Business of providing educational programs to persons engaged in underwater leisure activities with regard to matters, etc. necessary for underwater leisure activities;
  d. Other business prescribed by Presidential Decree as business equivalent to those specified in items a through c;
  9. The term "underwater leisure business entity" means a person who has filed for registration under Article 15 in order to conduct underwater leisure business;
  10. The term "underwater leisure instructor" means a person qualified under Article 11 (4) for safe underwater leisure activities as a person who can teach and guide persons engaged in underwater leisure activities.

Article 3 (Relationship to other statutes) Except as otherwise provided in any provision of the following Acts, this Act shall apply to underwater leisure activities:
  1. The Water-Related Leisure Activities Safety Act;
  2. The Excursion Ship and Ferry Business Act;
  3. The Installation and Utilization of Sports Facilities Act;
  4. The Fishing Management and Promotion Act;
  5. The Act on Prevention of Accidents at Coastal Sea.

CHAPTER II PROMOTION OF UNDERWATER LEISURE ACTIVITIES

Article 4 (Master plans for underwater leisure activities) (1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall formulate and implement a master plan for underwater leisure activities (hereinafter referred to as "master plan") every 5 years, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The master plan shall include the following matters:
  1. The basic direction for policies on safety and promotion of underwater leisure activities;
  2. Coordination and implementation of policies on safety and promotion of underwater leisure activities;
  3. Securing and management of funds for safety and promotion of underwater leisure activities;
  4. Installation, safety, maintenance, and management of facilities related to underwater leisure activities;
  5. Development of underwater leisure business;
  6. Education about underwater leisure activities;
  7. Collaboration among organizations related to underwater leisure activities;
  8. Surveys, research, and advertising for underwater leisure activities;
  9. Establishment and operation of an information system for underwater leisure activities;
  10. Other matters necessary for safety and promotion of underwater leisure activities.
  (3) When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to formulate a master plan, the Minister shall consult with the head of the relevant central administrative agency and the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor") thereon. The foregoing shall also apply where the Minister intends to modify the master plan.
  (4) When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to modify minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree in a master plan, the Minister may omit the consultation process under paragraph (3).
  (5) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the head of the relevant central administrative agency or the head of a local government to provide materials necessary for formulating, modifying, or implementing a master plan, or to offer cooperation; and such head shall comply with the request, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (6) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall notify the head of the relevant central administrative agency and a Mayor/Do Governor of a master plan formulated under paragraph (1).

Article 5 (Projects for promotion) In order to promote underwater leisure activities, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may conduct the following projects:
  1. Surveying, identifying, and promoting areas suitable for underwater leisure activities;
  2. Developing the manufacturing industry for underwater leisure;
  3. Formulating standards or guidelines for underwater leisure activities and education about underwater leisure;
  4. Holding events related to underwater leisure activities.

Article 6 (Voluntary agreement) An underwater leisure business entity may conclude an agreement with a fishing village association under Article 15 of the Fisheries Cooperatives Act.

CHAPTER III SAFETY MANAGEMENT AND DUTY OF COMPLIANCE

Article 7 (Establishment and enforcement of safety management regulations) (1) To prevent accidents that might occur in connection with underwater leisure activities, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall establish and enforce safety management regulations for underwater leisure (hereinafter referred to as "safety management regulations").
  (2) The safety management regulations shall include the following matters:
  1. Safety management of underwater leisure equipment and underwater leisure craft;
  2. Renting of underwater leisure equipment and use of underwater leisure craft;
  3. Safety management of underwater leisure facilities;
  4. Transportation and education of persons engaged in underwater leisure activities;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries for ensuring safety of persons engaged in underwater leisure activities.

Article 8 (Duty of compliance of persons engaged in underwater leisure activities) Persons engaged in underwater leisure activities shall comply with the following rules: <Amended on Jul. 25, 2023>
  1. Rules prescribed in Article 33 (3) of the Maritime Traffic Safety Act;
  2. Rules prescribed in Article 18 of the Fishery Resources Management Act;
  3. Persons shall wear underwater leisure equipment necessary for personal safety in accordance with the safety management regulations;
  4. No person shall leave the designated underwater leisure activity zone while engaging in underwater leisure activities.

Article 9 (Signs of underwater leisure activity zones and prohibition of navigation) (1) An underwater leisure business entity shall place signs prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries in an underwater leisure activity zone so that other ships, etc. can recognize the zone.
  (2) No ship shall navigate in an underwater leisure activity zone; provided, this shall not apply to any of the following ships:
  1. A ship navigating in an underwater leisure activity zone in order to rescue people or another ship;
  2. A ship navigating in an underwater leisure activity zone for a military operation or maritime security.
  (3) Even a ship specified in any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) shall endeavor to navigate safely as prescribed by Presidential Decree to protect persons engaged in underwater leisure activities.

Article 10 (Safety inspections) (1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall conduct a safety inspection on underwater leisure craft, underwater leisure equipment, underwater leisure facilities, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "underwater leisure craft, etc.") in an underwater leisure business establishment in accordance with the safety management regulations.
  (2) If the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries finds any problem in safety of underwater leisure craft, etc. as a result of a safety inspection conducted under paragraph (1), the Minister may issue an order to suspend the use of the underwater leisure craft, etc. or repair or restore the underwater leisure craft, etc. to the original state, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (3) A public official who conducts a safety inspection according to an order issued by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under paragraph (1) shall carry identification verifying his or her authority and shall present it to relevant persons.
  (4) Matters necessary for the guidelines, procedure, etc. for safety inspections under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 11 (Measures to be taken by business entities) (1) To ensure safety during underwater leisure activities, an underwater leisure business entity shall take the following measures: <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017; Oct. 22, 2024>
  1. Conducting safety inspections on underwater leisure craft, underwater leisure equipment, and other facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries;
  2. Monitoring weather and conditions of seawater or inland waters in each underwater leisure activity zone;
  3. Taking measures for rescue and relief when an accident occurs in connection with underwater leisure activities and reporting the occurrence of the accident to the relevant administrative agency, such as a police agency, a firefighting agency, or a coast guard agency;
  4. Taking measures to require persons engaged in underwater leisure activities to wear underwater leisure equipment and to receive safety education prior to boarding;
  5. Placing underwater leisure instructors within a place of business or boarding underwater leisure craft;
  6. Assigning underwater leisure instructors at underwater leisure activity training locations prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (2) Details necessary for the measures under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (3) A person seeking qualification as an underwater leisure instructor shall complete an educational course regarding the use and safety inspection of underwater leisure equipment, measures to respond to accidents in connection with underwater leisure activities, and other relevant matters.
  (4) Details necessary for the qualification of underwater leisure instructors, including the curriculum of the education under paragraph (3), shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 12 (Reporting of remote underwater leisure activities) (1) A person who intends to conduct an underwater leisure activity at a place at least 10 nautical miles far from the port of departure or coastline shall report his or her activity to the relevant police agency or coast guard agency. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) If a person dies, disappears, or sustains a serious injury as prescribed by Presidential Decree due to an accident in connection with an underwater leisure activity, a person engaged in the relevant underwater leisure business or engaged in the underwater leisure activity shall report the accident to the relevant administrative agency, such as a police agency, a firefighting agency, or a coast guard agency. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  (3) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (2), the head of the relevant administrative agency shall take measures necessary for search and rescue operations, accident response, and other related actions.
  (4) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, etc. for the reporting under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 13 (Restrictions on underwater leisure activities) (1) No person shall engage in underwater leisure activities from 30 minutes after sunset until 30 minutes before sunrise; provided, this shall not apply where any underwater leisure craft, etc. equipped with nighttime safety equipment and safety management personnel, are used, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may order persons engaged in underwater leisure activities to restrict the hours of underwater leisure activities, in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the condition of weather, seawater, or inland waters in the area near the relevant underwater leisure activity zone deteriorates;
  2. Where a marine accident occurs in an area near the relevant underwater leisure activity zone;
  3. Where various obstacles, such as fishing nets, exist in the sea;
  4. Where such restriction is deemed necessary for safety of underwater leisure activities.

Article 14 (Designation of underwater leisure activity prohibition zones) (1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may designate an underwater leisure activity prohibition zone after hearing opinions from the heads of relevant local governments for safety of underwater leisure activities, in any of the following cases:
  1. Where a harmful substance flows into the zone;
  2. Where a harmful living creature appears in the zone;
  3. Where a major sea route of ships goes through the zone;
  4. Where the relevant zone is recognized as impracticable to ensure safety for underwater leisure activities.
  (2) No person shall engage in underwater leisure activities in a prohibited zone designated under paragraph (1).
  (3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may revoke the designation of an underwater leisure activity prohibition zone under paragraph (1) after consulting with related agencies thereon.

CHAPTER IV UNDERWATER LEISURE BUSINESS

Article 15 (Registration of underwater leisure business) (1) A person who intends to conduct underwater leisure business shall meet the standards prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries and shall file for registration with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (2) If any registered matter is modified, an underwater leisure business entity that has filed for registration shall file for registration of modification, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 16 (Grounds for disqualification from registration of underwater leisure business) None of the following persons shall be qualified to file for registration of underwater leisure business:
  1. A minor, a person under the adult guardianship, or a person under the limited guardianship;
  2. A person for whom 2 years have not passed since his or her imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment declared by a court for violation of this Act was complete or exempted;
  3. A person who is under suspension of the execution of his or her imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment declared by a court for violation of this Act;
  4. A person for whom 2 years have not passed since his or her registration was revoked under Article 24 (excluding cases where registration was revoked on the ground of falling under the category specified in subparagraph 1 of this Article).

Article 17 (Succession to status of underwater leisure business entity) (1) Any of the following persons shall succeed to the status of an underwater leisure business entity:
  1. In the case of the death of an underwater leisure business entity, the heir;
  2. In the case where an underwater leisure business entity transfers the business, the transferee;
  3. In the case of a merger of a corporate underwater leisure business entity, the surviving corporation after the merger or the corporation newly established as a result of the merger.
  (2) The succession to the status of an underwater leisure business entity under paragraph (1) shall become effective on the day when the registration of the change is completed in accordance with Article 15 (2).

Article 18 (Reporting of temporary or permanent closure of business) (1) If an underwater leisure business entity intends to temporarily or permanently close the business or resume business operations during the registered period of business, the entity shall report thereon to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (2) Upon receipt of a report on temporary or permanent closure of business or resumption of business operations under paragraph (1), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall notify the tax office having jurisdiction over the domicile of the underwater leisure business of the temporary or permanent closure of business or resumption of business operations.

Article 19 (Usage fees) An underwater leisure business entity shall determine usage fees, including fares and rental charges, shall report the fees to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, and shall post the fees in a location plainly visible in the place of business. The foregoing shall also apply where it is intended to change any matter stated in the report.

Article 20 (Education) (1) A person who is engaged in underwater leisure business shall complete educational courses provided on a regular basis with regard to safety of underwater leisure activities, measures to respond to accidents in connection with underwater leisure activities, and other relevant matters.
  (2) Specific matters concerning the timing, curriculum, methods, etc. of the education under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 21 (Duty of compliance of underwater leisure business entities) (1) Underwater leisure business entities may use only the underwater leisure craft that have passed safety inspections under the Ship Safety Act, the Act on Registration and Inspection of Water Leisure Craft, and the Fishing Vessels Act for underwater leisure activities. <Amended on Jun. 10, 2022>
  (2) Underwater leisure craft for transporting persons engaged in underwater leisure activities shall be equipped with underwater leisure facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, including propeller shields and dive ladders.
  (3) No underwater leisure business entity shall conduct the following acts: <Amended on Jul. 25, 2023>
  1. Renting underwater leisure equipment or providing rides on underwater leisure craft to a person under 14 years of age who is not accompanied by a guardian, a person who is intoxicated as prescribed in Article 39 (4) of the Maritime Traffic Safety Act, a person suspected of being a mentally ill person defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Mental Health Act and deemed likely to pose a significant risk to their own or others’ safety (except where accompanied by a guardian), a person whose words or behavior are considered suspicious, or a person infected with a contagious disease;
  2. Loading an underwater leisure craft with people in excess of the capacity;
  3. Demanding money or valuables in addition to the usage fees posted under Article 19;
  4. Refusing to rent or operate an underwater leisure craft without good cause;
  5. Selling or providing alcoholic beverage in an underwater leisure craft or permitting a person engaged in underwater leisure activities to bring alcoholic beverage into an underwater leisure craft;
  6. Conducting any obscene act or an act harmful to morality;
  7. Continuing business operations during the hours in which underwater leisure activities are restricted under Article 13;
  8. Carrying or transporting any dangerous substance, such as any of the explosive and inflammable materials prescribed by Presidential Decree by an underwater leisure craft with persons engaged in underwater leisure activities on board;
  9. Dumping any petroleum product, excreta, or waste to the surface of seawater or inland waters, or underwater.

Article 22 (Restriction on business operations) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may order an underwater leisure business entity to restrict business hours or may temporarily suspend business operations, in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the condition of weather, seawater, or inland waters in the area near the relevant underwater leisure activity zone deteriorates;
  2. Where a marine accident occurs in an area near the relevant underwater leisure activity zone;
  3. Where various obstacles, such as fishing nets, exist in the sea;
  4. Where such restriction is deemed necessary for safety of underwater leisure activities.

Article 23 (Submission of materials) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may, if deemed necessary for the safety of underwater leisure activities, require an underwater leisure business entity to submit relevant documents or materials.

Article 24 (Revocation of registration of underwater leisure business) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may revoke the registration of underwater leisure business or may order an underwater leisure business entity to completely or partially suspend business operations for up to 3 months as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, in any of the following cases; provided, such registration shall be revoked in any of the cases specified in subparagraphs 1 through 3:
  1. Where the underwater leisure business entity has filed for registration by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the underwater leisure business entity falls under any category specified in the subparagraphs of Article 16;
  3. Where the underwater leisure business entity continues business operations even after the expiration of the period during which the occupancy or use of public waters is permitted under the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act;
  4. Where a person dies or is injured due to an intentional or grossly negligent act of the underwater leisure business entity or any of his or her employees;
  5. Where the underwater leisure business entity fails to file for registration of modification under Article 15 (2);
  6. Where the underwater leisure business entity violates any provision or order under Articles 19 through 22.

Article 25 (Penalty surcharges) (1) If the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries is to order a business suspension under Article 24, and such suspension is likely to cause significant inconvenience to users or harm the public interest, the Minister may impose a fine not exceeding 20 million won in lieu of the business suspension.
  (2) Matters necessary for the categories of violations subject to imposition of a penalty surcharge under paragraph (1), the amount of a penalty surcharge that may be imposed according to the degree of violation, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) If a person upon whom a penalty surcharge has been imposed under paragraph (1) fails to pay the penalty surcharge by the deadline, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall collect it in the same manner as delinquent national taxes are collected.

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 26 (Fees) A person who intends to file for registration of underwater leisure business or registration of modification under Article 15 shall pay fees to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 27 (Hearings) If the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to revoke registration of underwater leisure business under Article 24, the Minister shall hold a hearing.

Article 28 (Delegation of authority) Part of the authority of the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under this Act may be delegated to the head of an affiliated agency, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

